WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Every template has a contract, even when the contract is not written down.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:17.480
A template may assume that a type is movable, copyable, comparable, hashable, thread-safe, non-owning, owning, nothrow movable, cheaply copied, or safe to store.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:25.020
If those assumptions are unstated, callers may supply types that compile but do not behave as the template designer expected.

4
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.140
A generic contract should describe behavior, not only syntax.

5
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.760
It is not enough to say that a type must have an equality operator.

6
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:40.860
The code may also need equality to be stable, cheap enough for repeated use, consistent with hashing, and free of unexpected side effects.

7
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.760
Those requirements affect correctness and review.

8
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.560
Contracts should also explain ownership and lifetime.

9
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.100
A type accepted by value may be copied or moved.

10
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.920
A type accepted by reference may be borrowed only during the call.

11
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.420
A stored callable may run later and must not depend on a destroyed object.

12
00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.040
Generic code needs to make those expectations visible.

13
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.640
Documentation, constraints, tests, and review comments should tell the same story.

14
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.700
If documentation promises one behavior while the constraints allow a much broader set of types, the API invites misuse.

15
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.840
If tests cover only one type family, hidden assumptions remain untested.

16
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:31.920
Defensive generic design starts by asking, "what must be true about this type for the template to preserve its invariants?" The answer becomes the contract.

17
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.640
Contracts also help maintainers change code safely.

18
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:44.060
When requirements are visible, a future refactor can tell whether adding storage, comparison, hashing, or deferred execution changes what callers must provide.
